want to start with something a little  different today we have been talking for  a while about the idea of universal  basic income and whenever we talk about  universal basic income invariably we  also hear about some tech billionaire  that likes the idea of universal basic  income and we wonder anytime that we  hear about someone who is in such a  position of power that is for or against  some policy or idea we wonder well what  is it that motivates them to take that  position is it the good nature and  empathy and humanitarian Drive of tech  billionaires that has made some of the  notable ones say they support universal  basic income or is there more to it we  recently interviewed Andrew yang who's  running as a Democrat for president in  2020  he's running primarily on universal  basic income and we had a very good  conversation with him about ubi and he  didn't seem particularly concerned that  rich tech guys mostly as as they are  effectively are in favor of universal  basic income or at least didn't seem to  think that there were any self-serving  reasons for that and I've sort of  floated the idea that if you are Jeff  Bezos at Amazon or you are Mark  Zuckerberg at Facebook just to take two  specific examples and you imagine  everybody in the country getting an  extra $10,000 right some kind of ubi of  about $800 a month or $10,000 a year  people without that much extra money who  get an extra 10 grand a year are very  likely to spend that additional money on  stuff stuff like the products may be  sold on Amazon or may be stuff like the  products advertised on Facebook so  that's a possible motivation for some of  these tech billionaires to say I would  love to give people another $10,000 to  have which will become mostly spend  money on products because these are not  people likely to be saving a lot of that  money so let's put that aside for a  second but let's remember it let's now  talk about a related topic activism we  spoke  last week maybe the week before about  sort of the concept of approved and  acceptable activism and it was related  to the heckling of politicians and the  concept of sanctioned and comfortable  activism and as I've said before if you  were only going to be activists using  the tactics that those in power say are  okay you're unlikely to ever really get  anything done so the similarity here  would be tech billionaires and  politicians who say we want change we  want to improve the world etc except  only within the confines that they have  established and are comfortable with  that's the one important element that  underlies both how tech billionaires  want to change and improve the world and  how politicians want to encourage and  support activism so let me give you an  example when tech billionaires encourage  and approve of universal basic income  they support changing the world via  universal basic income in a very  specific way that's not going to disrupt  the broader status quo and it's going to  keep the infrastructure basically the  same and this is outlined in a really  good article in The New York Times by  Anand Anand Giridhar das I hope I'm  pronouncing that correctly called beware  rich people who say they want to change  the world what he outlines is that  giving ten thousand dollars to everybody  in the United States their universal  basic income changes nothing about  corporations being able to use loopholes  to pay no taxes it changes nothing about  the corporate lobbying industrial  complex that allows corporations to have  broad control over our political system  it doesn't do anything about eliminating  the carried interest loophole which is a  great tool for them to use to pay very  very little money it's not fake change  like universal basic income is a real  change and the ten thousand bucks a year  would be really great to a lot of people  but it is safe change that mis maintains  the status quo and doesn't endanger the  infrastructure that the corporate tech  billionaire elites  have built to benefit them right because  in most ubi plans now that money is  coming from money that we're currently  spending on social welfare programs so  things like food stamps Medicaid instead  of spending in that category where  there's a lot of bureaucracy we'll just  give the money directly to the people  now you could fund ubi by raising taxes  on the rich but that's not so  politically savvy and that's why we  don't see too many of those plans out  there sure but I think if you're a tech  billionaire you would probably be  willing to even if it did require paying  a little higher tax that's a pretty fair  trade for maintaining the exact status  quo system that has allowed you to  become a billionaire to begin with right  so now let's talk about the political  side politicians say let's raise the  minimum wage to $15 or $20 or let's  lower it to $10 or let's argue about the  effect of a higher minimum wage on  fast-food workers but basically what  they're all saying is let's keep the  system the same and argue about the  details which are 10 11 12 13 14 or $15  minimum wage business charity ok  business charity is another one where  you you see activism via donations  cheered but it's actually not going to  change the big picture of how the elites  and billionaires and corporations  control government and control  politicians so this is why I am more  steadfast than ever that we need to  decide the type of change that we want  we need to decide which protest methods  are appropriate and when right now I'm  not saying heck up heckling Sarah  Huckabee Sanders at a chicken joint  changes the status quo that that's not  what I'm saying it's not about the  heckling of Trump officials specifically  but the point is anytime you let them  set the rules it means the game will  never change you will be arguing about  exactly where the strike zone is rather  than actually changing the game that's  being played to use a sort of sports  analogy and right now really if you want  change you have to disrupt the status  quo that is where this sort of  fundamental discomfort or skepticism  comes from when you hear me say I  support you bi universal basic income  but we need to  understand why it seems to be so  appealing to so many tech billionaires  it doesn't mean that they're bad people  but we need to understand that a lot of  these movements it's sort of it's a  control I don't like to use the term  controlled opposition but it is an  arbitrarily narrowed scope of activism  you bi- income tax for example as a  discussion  neither changes the system that has  allowed corporations to come to  completely subjugate subjugate and take  over control of our political system  that i think is the key point to keep in  mind here today's show was made possible  in part by sion games and their new game  for phone and tablet called antidote  which is an award winning strategy game  it's all about using vaccines to defend  the human body from bacteria and viruses  it's fast paced the graphics are amazing  the game has actually been commended by  the World Health Organization because it  has a mission to fight back against the  anti vaccine misinformation so the game  is educational and fun to play download  antidote for free on your phone or  tablet in the Google Play Store or the  iOS App Store and have a blast using  vaccines and the body's immune system to  fight off a deadly diseases and don't  forget to search for scion games on  Twitter Facebook and Instagram if you're  watching this on YouTube I've put the  links under the video

